TI  - Effects of indoor temperature cycles on human subjective thermal perception, physiological responses and cognitive performance under flexible air-conditioning operation
T2  - BUILDING AND ENVIRONMENT
LA  - English
KW  - Temperature cycles
KW  - Subjective thermal perception
KW  - Physiological response
KW  - Cognitive performance
KW  - DEMAND RESPONSE
KW  - COMFORT
KW  - STRATEGIES
KW  - MEMORY
AB  - Building energy consumption accounts for approximately 30% of global energy demand, and flexible airconditioning operation can effectively reduce building operational energy consumption. At present, the flexible scheduling of air-conditioning is often accompanied by indoor temperature cycles. Two temperature cycle conditions (25-29 degrees C, at 4 degrees C/h and 8 degrees C/h, corresponding to cycle periods of 120 min and 60 min, respectively) and a steady-state control condition (26 degrees C) were designed to evaluate the acceptability of temperature cycles. Thirty healthy college students (18 males and 12 females) were recruited, and each participant completed all the experimental conditions. Subjective votes, physiological responses and cognitive performance of the subjects were measured. The results show that compared with the steady-state condition, thermal sensation vote (TSV) under temperature cycle conditions was within the acceptable range, heart rate variability (males only) showed no significant difference, memory was unaffected, and attention was improved. Temperature change direction significantly influenced thermal comfort. At the same air temperature (Ta), TSV during the ramp-down phase was lower than that during the ramp-up phase. The results suggest a possible thermal habituation effect under the 8 degrees C/h cycling condition, with lower TSV and a slight decrease in mean skin temperature during the second cycle. The relationships of TSV with Ta and dTa/dt were established, along with the thermal comfort zone corresponding to a TSV of 0 to 0.85. This study can provide valuable support for the formulation and implementation of flexible air-conditioning operation strategies.
