AB  - Low-frequency components of floor impact noise can increase resident burden, but their autonomic and electroencephalography (EEG) network correlates remain unclear. This study examined within-source low-frequency enrichment of three floor-impact-noise playback configurations (Ball1, Ball4, and Running) at -12, 0, and +12 dB in the 25-50 Hz band. Forty-five adults completed a repeated-measures listening experiment; EDA/BVPderived HRV and EEG analyses included 36 and 37 participants, respectively. The targeted band-gain manipulation produced coordinated differences in low-frequency-sensitive, broadband, and loudness-related descriptors, allowing the delivered acoustic conditions to be examined across subjective, autonomic, and EEG outcomes. Enrichment increased annoyance across all sources. Source-averaged HRV endpoints decreased with stronger enrichment, whereas phasic EDA responses varied by source and showed the clearest increasing tendency in Ball1. Higher annoyance was associated with reduced posterior alpha organization, reduced alpha-band graphlevel integration, and an anatomically informed source-ROI estimate of increased insula-related theta. SensorROI visualization also varied across source and acoustic contexts, with FDR-significant Ball1 theta increases in global efficiency and mean node strength. Noise sensitivity increased overall burden, and exploratory AI benchmarking distinguished strict generalization from within-distribution performance. Together, the findings show that low-frequency-enriched floor-impact noise is expressed across perceptual, autonomic, and neural levels and support a resident-centered multimodal assessment framework.
